    {
      "width": 320,
      "visibleExistingItem": "sugar",
      "reading": {
        "text": "In his 1657 account of Barbados, Richard Ligon describes a sugar plantation sale that included enslaved people among its property. His projected plantation budget treats enslaved people as assets to be maintained.\n\nThis is one record of the coerced labour behind seventeenth-century Atlantic plantation sugar. Ligon wrote from the colonists’ profit-seeking perspective, not the voices of the people they enslaved. It is not a history of every place or period in which sugar was made.",
        "attribution": "Game-author paraphrase and contextual note, not a quotation. Richard Ligon, A True & Exact History of the Island of Barbados (1657), pp. 22–23 and 113–116. A colonial participant’s account; not testimony from enslaved workers."
      },
      "stageMotivation": "The orchard has sent more ripe fruit than we can serve fresh. Make tomorrow’s marmalade and tonight’s jam tart and crumble; the preserving works also wants a fruit syrup sample. Sugar has an optional historical note about enslaved plantation labour in seventeenth-century Barbados.",
      "readStateMutation": false
    },
